#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105887 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e34a506-206c-5a51-9251-f1cd31df64d4" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Surface Integrals"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105888 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65e5d5ea-f579-5871-924d-075a207c9aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Surface Integrals Using Polar Coordinates"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105889 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8885504f-07fb-5308-a982-608d7baa262b" :knowledge-point/title "Express Surface Integrals as Repeated Integrals"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107153 :tutorial/content "Recall that for a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v) \\in D\\subseteq \\mathbb R^2,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\left\\|  \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\right\\|  \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nNow, if a surface is given by the explicit equation $z=g(x,y)$ over a plane region $D$ in Cartesian coordinates, then the canonical parametrization of this surface is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(x,y) = \\langle x,\\:  y, \\: g(x,y) \\rangle, \\qquad (x,y) \\in D.\n$$\n\nMoreover, we know that under the canonical parametrization, the magnitude of the fundamental vector product is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{y}^{′}‖ & =\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D\\subseteq \\mathbb R^2,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D f\\left(x,y,g(x,y)\\right) \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "411ccf43-163d-53bb-b1f4-2b6832c905e1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7650 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
